; TEST: expect-exit=100 c-emit-exit=4
;
; THE FIXTURE THAT MAKES THE SHAPES DIFFERENT. Twenty f32 contributions, the
; same twenty in the same order, reduced twice -- `ordinata` and
; `arborea 8` -- and the two answers are compared as BIT PATTERNS, not as
; floats. They differ, which is the entire reason spec 5.4 has two shapes
; and ADR 0012 makes the reference lowering their definition: a reduction's
; rounding tree is observable, so it must be specified, and the way to
; specify it is to pin the bits.
;
; THE VALUES: 1.0f once, then nineteen copies of 2**-24 (half an ulp of
; 1.0f), so groups are [0..8), [8..16) and the tail [16..20) -- spec 5.4's
; own worked shape, with 20 not a multiple of 8 on purpose: the tail is
; exactly what the spec left undefined and this defines.
;
;   ordinata    acc = 0.0; +1.0 -> 1.0; then each +2**-24 is a TIE rounded
;               to even, back to 1.0, nineteen times over
;                                                 = 1.0      0x3F800000
;   arborea 8   group1 = tree[1.0, e,e,e,e,e,e,e]
;                      = ((1.0+e) + (e+e)) + ((e+e) + (e+e))
;                      = (1.0 + 2**-23) + 2**-22 = 1 + 3 ulp   0x3F800003
;               acc = 0.0 + group1                = 1 + 3 ulp
;               group2 = tree[e x 8] = 8e = 2**-21 = 4 ulp     0x35000000
;               acc                               = 1 + 7 ulp
;               group3 = tree[e x 4] = 4e = 2**-22 = 2 ulp     0x34800000
;                        (the TAIL, folded by redfin)
;               acc                               = 1 + 9 ulp  0x3F800009
;
; Every pairwise sum above is exact in f32 except the first, `1.0 + e`,
; which is the tie; that is why the shapes diverge at all, and why the
; divergence is 9 ulps rather than luck.
;
; The two expected bit patterns were COMPUTED by this reference, which is
; ssa-ir.md 2.6's definition transcribed and nothing else:
;
;     import struct
;     def f32(x): return struct.unpack("f", struct.pack("f", x))[0]
;     def bits32(x): return struct.unpack("I", struct.pack("f", x))[0]
;     def ordinata(vals, ident=0.0):
;         acc = ident
;         for v in vals: acc = f32(acc + v)
;         return acc
;     def tree(cells):                  # adjacent pairs, odd trailing up
;         k = len(cells); s = list(cells); stride = 1
;         while stride < k:
;             i = 0
;             while i + stride < k:
;                 s[i] = f32(s[i] + s[i + stride]); i += 2 * stride
;             stride *= 2
;         return s[0]
;     def arborea(vals, w, ident=0.0):
;         acc, held = ident, []
;         for v in vals:
;             held.append(v)
;             if len(held) == w:
;                 acc = f32(acc + tree(held)); held = []
;         if held: acc = f32(acc + tree(held))
;         return acc
;     VALS = [1.0] + [2.0 ** -24] * 19
;     bits32(ordinata(VALS))  -> 0x3F800000 = 1065353216
;     bits32(arborea(VALS, 8)) -> 0x3F800009 = 1065353225
;
; HOW THE BITS ARE READ. `store f32` then `load u32` from the same slot --
; a float in memory is its raw IEEE bit pattern and the path is mov-shaped
; (ssa-ir.md 2.7; tests/ir/float_mem.ir pins that settlement), so the
; `cmp.eq u32` sees the pattern itself. `fcmp.eq` would not do: it cannot
; tell 1.0 from 1.0 + 9 ulp being wrong by 9 ulp rather than by nothing,
; and it cannot see a sign at all.
;
;   check 1  ordinata over the twenty  = 0x3F800000
;   check 2  arborea 8 over the twenty = 0x3F800009
;
; Mutation (run): the tail folded as a LEFT-TO-RIGHT sum into the
; accumulator instead of a tree -> check 2 fails (four copies of e added one
; at a time to a 1+7ulp accumulator round away, giving 0x3F800007). The group
; folded into acc right to left, or the groups folded in reverse index order
; -> check 2 fails.
;
; One mutation these twenty values do NOT catch, named rather than implied:
; padding the tail out to w with the identity and running the full w-tree
; gives the same answer HERE, because the four tail cells are equal and
; positive (tree[e,e,e,e,0,0,0,0] = 4e = tree[e,e,e,e]). It is observable
; when a tail cell is -0.0, since 0.0 + -0.0 is +0.0 -- the same edge
; red_ordinata_f32.ir's check 1 pins for the identity fold -- and no fixture
; here puts a -0.0 in a tail `[UNTESTED]`.
;
; `c-emit-exit=4`: the C backend refuses the three reduction opcodes by name
; (c-backend.md D4 rows 36-38), so the differential phase checks parity of
; that refusal. When it gains them, this fixture is the cross-check that the
; two backends agree bit for bit on both shapes.

functio @initium (ptr) -> u8 {
b0:
%0 = param ptr 0
%1 = slot 8 8
%2 = fconst f32 1065353216
%3 = fconst f32 864026624
%4 = redinit f32 fadd ordinata 0
contrib %4 %2
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
contrib %4 %3
%5 = redfin f32 %4
store f32 %1 0 nativus %5
%6 = load u32 %1 0 nativus
%7 = iconst u32 1065353216
%8 = cmp.eq u32 %6 %7
br %8 b2 b1
b1:
%9 = iconst u8 1
ret %9
b2:
%10 = redinit f32 fadd arborea 8
contrib %10 %2
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
contrib %10 %3
%11 = redfin f32 %10
store f32 %1 0 nativus %11
%12 = load u32 %1 0 nativus
%13 = iconst u32 1065353225
%14 = cmp.eq u32 %12 %13
br %14 b4 b3
b3:
%15 = iconst u8 2
ret %15
b4:
%16 = iconst u8 100
ret %16
}
